\section{How it works}{

Around each cell, terrain is sampled on a ring rather than along rays to
the horizon:
\itemize{
\item Sample points sit between \code{min_rad} and \code{max_rad} out, stepping
by \code{rad_inc}, and every \code{angular_res} degrees around the full circle -
by default 11 distances times 24 directions, so 264 samples per cell.
Each is rounded to the nearest pixel.
\item The observer's eye is placed at the cell's elevation plus
\code{observer_height}. Samples at or above eye level contribute nothing; each
one below adds its drop below the eye divided by its distance.
\item Each contribution is weighted in proportion to the area of the ring it
came from, so distant rings - which contain more ground - are not
drowned out by the handful of pixels close in.
\item The total is divided by what perfectly flat ground would produce, which
is what puts flat terrain at exactly 1.
}

Distances here are in map units, as everywhere else, so the same settings
mean the same ground distances on any DEM. Computation is at native
resolution, tile by tile, with tiles overlapping by \code{max_rad}.
}

\section{Tuning}{

\itemize{
\item \code{min_rad} and \code{max_rad} set the band of feature sizes the result responds
to, and they are in \strong{map units} (metres, normally): the defaults cover
10-20 m on any DEM. Widen the band for larger landforms.
\item Starting at \code{min_rad} rather than at the cell itself is deliberate - it
keeps immediate micro-relief and DEM noise from dominating. Lower it to
pick up smaller features, raise it to ignore more local roughness.
\item \code{angular_res} controls how many directions are sampled (360 divided by
it). Smaller values give a smoother, less spoke-like result and cost
proportionally more time.
\item \code{rad_inc} controls sampling density along each direction. Larger steps
are faster and coarser.
\item \code{observer_height} sets how sensitive the result is: a low observer
exaggerates small height differences, a tall one flattens the contrast
because everything already lies well below the eye. The 1.7 m default is
roughly human eye level.
}
